      title: "Нужно ли закрыть центр города для машин?",
      text: "Should private cars be banned from city centres?\n\nIn recent years, cities from Oslo to Madrid have introduced measures to reduce the number of cars in their historic centres, and several others are considering going further and banning private vehicles completely. Supporters see this as the key to more liveable cities, while opponents fear it will damage businesses and make life harder for those who depend on their cars. Both sides deserve a fair hearing.\n\nThe arguments in favour are strong. Firstly, car-free centres are cleaner and quieter. Air pollution and noise pollution are linked to serious health problems, and the first people to benefit from cleaner air are those who live and work in the inner city. Furthermore, removing cars frees up an enormous amount of space. Streets that were once used for parking can become cycle lanes, pedestrianised squares or green spaces where children can play safely. What is more, experience shows that once people get used to the change, local shops often benefit, because pedestrians tend to stop, browse and spend more than drivers who simply pass through.\n\nOn the other hand, a complete ban raises genuine concerns. Not everyone can walk or cycle long distances. Elderly people, parents with small children and people with disabilities may find a car-free centre much harder to reach, particularly if public transport is overcrowded or unreliable. It could also be argued that a ban punishes commuters from the commuter belt, many of whom moved there because housing in the centre was not affordable. Finally, some shop owners claim that customers who cannot drive will simply go to large out-of-town shopping centres instead.\n\nOn balance, I believe that reducing cars in city centres is the right direction, but a sudden, total ban is not. Although the benefits for health and quality of life are clear, a ban will only work if the alternatives work first. Cities should invest in reliable public transport and park-and-ride schemes, make exceptions for those who genuinely need a car, and introduce changes step by step. Despite the difficulties, a centre designed for people rather than vehicles is a goal worth pursuing.",
